media: h713-hdmirx: S_DV_TIMINGS refuses a ring no slot can hold

Since 0136k the capability cap is the firmware's own mode table, up to
4096x2160 at 297 MHz, because the ring rule of 0136f is what keeps the plane
safe and not the cap. That is right for QUERY_DV_TIMINGS, where the ring
comes from INCAP's rowbyte and the firmware never writes a ring larger than
a slot. It left one door open on the way in, though: S_DV_TIMINGS takes a
timing by hand and sets the ring from it directly,

	ring.width  = t->bt.width;
	ring.height = t->bt.height;
	ring.pitch  = ALIGN(t->bt.width, H713_INCAP_ROWBYTE_UNIT);

with no INCAP in sight, so S_DV_TIMINGS(4096x2160) now succeeds and hands
the queue a plane size of 8 847 360 bytes against a slot of 2 093 056.
Nothing overruns - h713_hdmirx_alloc() checks the size against the slot
pitch and holds - but the refusal comes out of REQBUFS as -EINVAL, three
calls later and in a place where it reads like a driver bug rather than an
answer (T2 report 8.1).

So the check moves to where the decision is made: one comparison of pitch
times height against H713_HDMIRX_SLOT_PITCH, -ERANGE, and one line naming
both numbers. -ERANGE is what S_DV_TIMINGS already answers for a timing the
cap refuses, so nothing new has to be learned by a caller.

h713-tv is not affected either way: it uses QUERY, never S_DV_TIMINGS.

Follow-up to 0136k, which stays byte-identical. Source: umbau/work/T2/
REPORT.txt section 8.1, doku/128 section 3 item 7.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff -ruN a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
@@ -3999,6 +3999,26 @@ static int h713_hdmirx_s_dv_timings(struct file *file, void *priv,
 	ring.height = t->bt.height;
 	ring.pitch = ALIGN(t->bt.width, H713_INCAP_ROWBYTE_UNIT);
 
+	/*
+	 * A capture slot is H713_HDMIRX_SLOT_PITCH bytes and the plane cannot
+	 * be told to write anywhere else, so a hand-given timing whose ring
+	 * does not fit one is not a format this device can have. Until 0136k
+	 * the capability cap refused those timings first; since the cap
+	 * follows the firmware's table, S_DV_TIMINGS(4096x2160) gets this far
+	 * and the refusal used to come out of REQBUFS as -EINVAL, which reads
+	 * like a driver bug instead of an answer (T2 report 8.1). QUERY is not
+	 * affected: there the ring comes from the rowbyte, and the firmware
+	 * never writes a ring larger than a slot.
+	 */
+	if ((u64)ring.pitch * ring.height > H713_HDMIRX_SLOT_PITCH) {
+		dev_info(rx->dev,
+			 "%ux%u needs %llu bytes a slot, the capture has %u\n",
+			 ring.width, ring.height,
+			 (u64)ring.pitch * ring.height,
+			 (unsigned int)H713_HDMIRX_SLOT_PITCH);
+		return -ERANGE;
+	}
+
 	h713_hdmirx_set_timings(rx, t, &ring);
 
 	return 0;
